# Step 11 - cache-service (Tactic B - compressed)
# Fetch the SAME payload WITH gzip and compare against step 10.

@cache = http://localhost:8086
@region = Europe

### Gzipped read
GET {{cache}}/api/cache/articles/{{region}}
Accept: application/json
Accept-Encoding: gzip

# What to look for:
#   - response header "Content-Encoding: gzip"  <- proof the server compressed it
#   - smaller bytes-on-the-wire than step 10 (less network = greener)
#
# Note: some HTTP clients auto-decompress and then display the FULL size, so the
# size readout may look the same even though the transfer was compressed. The
# reliable proof in-client is the "Content-Encoding: gzip" header. For exact
# byte-for-byte numbers, use scripts/gaf-demo.ps1 (it measures with curl), e.g.:
#   curl -s -o NUL -w "%{size_download}" -H "Accept-Encoding: identity" {{cache}}/api/cache/articles/{{region}}
#   curl -s -o NUL -w "%{size_download}" -H "Accept-Encoding: gzip"     {{cache}}/api/cache/articles/{{region}}
